FILE: vUX-skeleton.js
FEATURE: skeleton-module
DATE: 2026-10-05
PRIOR STATE: as left by feature 'timeline-list-verification' (uncommitted at the time)

REASON
NEW FILE. The Skeleton module: placeholder screens shaped like the content about
to arrive.

vUX had ProgressIndicator, which says that something is loading. Nothing said
what shape it would take. The design point that sets this module apart from a
generic skeleton library is that the consumer already wrote the shape. Component
and DataView templates tag every dynamic node with data-v-field / data-v-bind, so
the skeleton is DERIVED from that template instead of being drawn a second time.

Design points worth recording:

  - Two modes, chosen by config.template. Template mode clones the template into
    ghosts inside a LAYER, a shallow copy of the container inserted before it,
    and hides the container. The layer copies the container's class/style so a
    grid lays out the ghosts like the real items. Ghosts never enter the
    container, because Component.sync() and DataView's reconcile own every child
    of it and would treat a ghost as a vanished row. Mask mode (no template)
    masks existing content in place and records every change so unmask
    reverses it exactly.

  - Timing is part of the contract. show() waits config.delay (120ms) before
    drawing, so a fast load never flashes. Once drawn, hide() keeps the skeleton
    up until config.minDuration (400ms) has passed, so a slow load never
    flickers. hide() returns a promise for that reason. during() wraps the flow
    and counts overlapping calls.

  - show() during a hide in flight queues a fresh show after the hide instead of
    cancelling it, because the caller awaiting the hide expects the skeleton to
    be gone when its promise resolves.

  - Validation runs while the setting call is on the stack: data-skeleton values
    when config.template is set (or on each show() in mask mode), and the
    detached-container check in show(). Otherwise a bad value would throw from
    inside the delay timer, where nobody can catch it.

  - Replaced elements (img, video, svg, canvas…) are swapped for a <span> with
    the same class/style in ghosts, because an <img> without src paints a
    broken-image icon and cannot carry the fill.

  - Empty fields get bars of explicit em width (WIDTHS, varied so a column does
    not read as a ruled grid). Single-line bars are inline-block so that two
    fields sharing a line, such as an author and a timestamp, stay on one line.
    Text that already has words is wrapped in an inline span: an inline
    background covers each line's run of text and nothing more, so the bars
    are exactly as long as the lines. Mask mode puts the SAME text nodes back
    on unmask.

  - Invisible filler is non-breaking spaces, never visible characters, so a
    ghost drawn before the stylesheet arrives shows nothing stray.

  - The stylesheet is linked at import and appended immediately, rather than
    through linkStyleSheet() from initialize(). That helper waits 300ms before
    it appends, and a skeleton is by nature needed in a page's first moments.
    The first test run showed ghosts unstyled because of that delay.

  - Accessibility: the layer is aria-hidden + inert, the masked container is
    inert (its previous inert value is restored), the container carries
    aria-busy, and config.label is announced through a visually hidden
    role="status" element.

Verified in headless Chrome against a scratch harness (27 checks: state machine,
delay, minDuration, fast-hide-never-shows, during() value and rejection, ghost
derivation, attribute stripping, layer/teardown cleanliness, byte-for-byte mask
reversal, destroy, and every validation error).
